ement last Friday do you  not take responsibility for that no we  we both know that we're seeing the same  impacts of Putin's war in Ukraine  cascading through things like the cost  of energy some of the supply side  implications of that and that's  impacting every major economy and just  the same every major economy you're  seeing interest rates going up as well  so as as we're not to the same degree as  in the UK  every major economy is dealing with  exactly these same issues at the heart  of this all is a deep tension and one  that is now playing out in financial  markets on one part of town the  chancellor has a stated growth objective  of two and a half percent on the other  part of town the bank of England's own  view is that the growth potential of the  UK economy is about one percent below  that that tension is now what we're  seeing being resolved in financial  markets because they're saying well if  if the government wants to go for two  and a half percent or the chancellor  does  that is going to mean the interest rates  are going to have to rise in a way  that's going to defeat the objective for  two and a half percent  it is hard to think of any other budget  which has caused quite such chaos a  currency plunge followed by a mortgage  Spike and now a threat to the very  Plumbing that sits beneath our economy  what happens next is anyone's guess
 

